## Introduction
Zinc is among the most biologically important trace metals. In the past 60 years more than 300 enzymes have been identified in which zinc plays a catalytic role. Within the past 15 years, the importance of zinc as a structural cofactor has been confirmed with the identification of hundreds of proteins that possess one or more zinc-stabilized structural motifs. Perhaps the best known of these diverse structures is the classical ${\mathrm{Cys}}_{2}{\mathrm{His}}_{2}$ zinc-finger motif, which comprises a distinct superfamily of zinc-stabilized protein structure elements. ${\mathrm{Cys}}_{2}{\mathrm{His}}_{2}$ zinc-finger domains were identified first in transcription factor IIIA (TFIIIA) [1,2], and in fact, most of the proteins that possess ${\mathrm{Cys}}_{2}{\mathrm{His}}_{2}$ zinc fingers are eukaryotic transcription factor proteins involved in nucleicacid binding and the regulation of gene expression. While several studies have offered remarkable insight into the details of zinc-finger–nucleic-acid interactions (see [3•,4,5]), the primary focus of this review will center on studies of the coupled processes of metal binding and protein folding involving zinc-finger (and other zinc-binding) domains.
The relative simplicity and the autonomous nature of zinc fingers make them a particularly useful model system in studies of metal binding and protein folding. Zinc fingers represent a distinct protein structure element that depends solely upon the presence of a bound metal ion for proper folding and, as a result, relatively small peptides capable of adopting a particular well-defined secondary structure can be studied independently of bulk protein. Additionally, because zinc-finger peptides are small (typically ${\sim}3\mathrm{\kDa}$ ) they can be synthesized easily and studied using a variety of biophysical techniques. The studies discussed here employ zinc-finger peptides, of both native and designed sequences, to investigate the nature of metal binding and the implications of metal binding on the overall folding and conformational stability of zinc-finger structures.
As mentioned above, zinc-finger domains were first proposed from studies of the nucleic-acid binding protein TFIIIA. The zinc requirement for DNA-binding activity of this transcription factor had been demonstrated as early as 1983 [6], but it was examination of the primary sequence of TFIIIA that led to the zinc-finger hypothesis. The observation of nine tandem repeats of a general sequence characterized by uniformly spaced metal-binding (2 Cys, 2 His) and hydrophobic amino-acid residues led to the proposal that each sequence repeat bound one zinc ion and folded into an independent structural domain [2,7]. This general sequence, (Phe, $\mathrm{Tyr})\mathrm{-X\mathrm{-}C y s-X\it{2\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}C y s-X\it{3\mathrm{-}P h e-}}}$ $\mathrm{X}_{5}\mathrm{-Leu-X}_{2}\mathrm{-His-X}_{3,4}\mathrm{-His-X}_{2-6}$ (where X is any amino acid) now refers to a superfamily of TFIIIA-like zinc finger structures termed ‘classical’ zinc fingers, which appear to represent the most abundant nucleic-acid-binding motif in eukaryotic transcription factors.
Naturally, the earliest studies of the metal-binding properties of zinc fingers centered on peptides corresponding to finger sequences from TFIIIA and similar classical zincfinger peptides. Circular dichroism studies of zinc-finger domains from TFIIIA [8] and the transcription factor ADR1 [9] revealed a unique feature of zinc-finger domains. The circular-dichroism spectrum of the apo (metal free) form of the peptide changes drastically upon the addition of metals; this is because the finger peptide is relatively unstructured in the absence of bound metal and protein folding is dependent on metal binding. Structural characterization of zinc-finger peptides using NMR techniques quickly revealed that this relatively short chain of amino acids folds into an independent structure stabilized by bound zinc, with additional stability afforded by packing the sidechains of the conserved hydrophobic residues into a ‘hydrophobic core’ [10,11].
These fundamentals are recapitulated to varying degrees in other zinc-binding proteins, including proteins with more structurally complex zinc-binding sites and zinc metalloenzymes, such as carbonic anhydrase. As the binding proteins increase in size and complexity, additional protein interactions external to the zinc-binding site become increasingly important. Recent studies have begun to probe these second-shell considerations and are reviewed below.
## Protein-folding studies
The autonomous nature of the zinc-finger domain led to its use in studies aimed at understanding the complex processes involved in protein folding. That metal binding is so closely associated with protein folding in these systems, coupled with the fact that other spectroscopically active metals, such as $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ , can be substituted for the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion at the binding site, makes these complexes useful models for evaluating protein-folding thermodynamics and the contributions of individual amino-acid residues to overall protein structure. This area was reviewed quite thoroughly by Berg and Godwin [12] in 1997 and will be summarized here within the context of more recent advances.
## Thermodynamics of protein folding
Preliminary studies of the protein-folding dynamics of zincfinger domains involved modification of the hydrophobic residues that are highly conserved in classical zinc-finger sequences. Two-dimensional NMR studies by Weiss and co-workers [13–15] revealed that although the overall $\upbeta.$ -strand–turn– $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\upbeta}$ -strand– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{a}$ -helix $(\upbeta\upbeta\upalpha)$ structure motif of the peptide was retained, mutations in the hydrophobic core disrupted the overall stability of the folded peptide.
Berg and co-workers [16] took the opposite approach to investigate the role of the hydrophobic residues, by the construction of a minimalist zinc finger (MZF). In the MZF peptide, all amino-acid residues that were not conserved in the general sequence (i.e. all residues except the four metal-binding residues and the three conservedhydrophobic residues) were changed to alanine. The authors were able to demonstrate that MZF adopts a secondary structure similar to that of other classical zinc-finger peptides on metal binding, suggesting that metal binding and hydrophobic packing are sufficient for proper folding.
These studies demonstrated very early on and for a very simple system the importance of second-shell interactions on the nature of the zinc binding site. The importance of second-shell contributions to more complex zinc-binding sites, such as the catalytic site of the zinc metalloenzyme carbonic anhydrase, are discussed below. It is also important to point out, however, that much work has been done to examine the importance of the direct zinc ligands on protein folding and the overall stability of the folded finger.
Studies of viral zinc-dependent RNA-binding proteins have shown a possible limit of affinity. The nucleocapsidbinding protein from HIV-1 and the COM protein from the lysogenic strain phage Mu both show very high zinc-binding affinities: for COM, $K_{\mathrm{Zn}}$ is around $10^{13}\mathrm{M^{-1}}$ [17]; for the nucleocapsid-binding protein, $K_{\mathrm{Zn}}$ is around $10^{14}~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ [18,19]. It may be that such high affinities allow a rapidly reproducing virus to compete for a limiting zinc pool.
There is a second surprising aspect of these large bindingconstants. In such cases, binding is generally determined by metal-ion competition experiments employing the protein and a well-characterized small molecule (i.e. $N.$ -hydroxy-ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid [HEDTA]). A cursory analysis of the kinetic aspects of binding reveals that some complexity must exist within the mechanisms of metal binding and dissociation. A large binding constant $(K_{\mathrm{B,Zn}}\sim10^{14}\mathrm{M}^{-1})$ can be treated as a ratio of the rates of binding and dissociation (i.e. $[k_{\mathrm{on}}/k_{\mathrm{off}}]=K_{\mathrm{B}})$ . The ‘on rate’ must be diffusion-limited or slower (i.e. $k_{o n}<10^{9}\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . As $(k_{o n}/k_{o f f})=K_{\mathrm{B}}$ , and $K_{\mathrm{B}}=10^{14}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ , it follows that $k_{o f f}\leq10^{-5}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ . This low value for $k_{o f f}$ suggests a dissociation half-time of many hours: in fact, equilibrium is reached in seconds. It follows, then, that the process of metal exchange involves discrete (ternary) intermediates. ‘Mixed ligand’ intermediates are well-established for exchange with small molecules. There is some evidence that ‘mixed ligand’ complexes might exist between two protein molecules as well.
Recently, Raman spectroscopy has been utilized to examine the folding pathway of a synthetic peptide corresponding to the third zinc finger of mouse transcription factor Zif268 [20•]. Cooperative binding of the cysteine and histidine residues to the zinc ion is essential for adopting the $\upbeta\upbeta\upalpha$ structural motif. In addition, the arrangement of these direct zinc-ligands appears to be important, particularly in helix formation. By monitoring Raman marker bands of metal–His binding, Miura et al. [21] demonstrated that the folding process involves $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}^{2+}}$ coordination first to the cysteine ligands followed by a $\upbeta$ -strand $\scriptstyle\to\alpha.$ -helix structural transition that accompanies histidine binding $\left[20^{\bullet}\right]$ . Rearrangement of the ligands (i.e. substitution of cysteine for histidine and histidine for cysteine) hinders the folding transition, rendering the peptide incapable of adopting the classical finger structure.
## Protein-design studies
The modular behavior of zinc-finger domains has also contributed to their utility in studies directed toward protein design. The construction of novel proteins with predetermined three-dimensional structure and well-defined functionality represents an exciting challenge. Although the incorporation of functional metal-binding moieties into native protein structures has met with some success (see $[22^{\bullet\bullet}])$ , this area of research remains plagued by what is generally referred to as ‘the protein-folding problem.’ We simply lack a complete understanding of the complex relationship between primary sequence, tertiary structure and protein functionality.
Zinc-finger motifs are important in this pursuit because they represent one extreme in the range of folding behaviors exhibited by metalloproteins. The well-defined structure of zinc-finger domains is completely dependent on metal binding. Thus, the incorporation of such domains into non-metalloproteins can be used to impart new structural and spectroscopic properties on a previously well-understood protein. The ultimate goal is to employ this methodology to systematically bestow desired functionality on a native protein.
A clearer understanding of the processes involved in protein folding can lead to the development of better strategies for de novo protein design. In fact, two independent groups have reported the construction of independently folding polypeptide motifs based on the zinc finger template [23–26]. In both cases, the zinc-binding ligands were replaced with residues extending the hydrophobic interaction network in the core of the finger structure. Dahiyat and Mayo [23,24] used their sidechainrepacking algorithm to create an independently folding ${\upbeta\upbeta\upalpha}$ motif through a fully automated protein-design process. The design approach of Struthers and co-workers [25,26] involved the use of modified amino acids: a constrained dipeptide sequence, D-Pro–Ser, was incorporated to stabilize the $\upbeta$ -turn and a bulky, hydrophobic residue, 3-(1,10-phenanthrol-2-yl)-L-Ala, was introduced at the former metal-binding site [25]. Subsequently, the authors were able to modify their design strategy to employ several natural amino acids while retaining the overall ${\upbeta\upbeta\upalpha}$ structure of the peptide in the absence of metal binding. These studies ultimately achieve a similar result, designing a relatively short peptide with somewhat predictable folding behavior, and they represent important yet diverse accomplishments in the field of protein design.
Several studies have reported the incorporation of designed metal-binding sites into proteins that do not possess such sites in their native form [27–29]. In cases where these metal binding sites were patterned after zinc-finger binding sites (composed of cysteine and histidine residues) the absorption spectra of the $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ -bound forms of these proteins were consistent with tetrahedral metal-coordination, and the metal ion affinities were only one to two orders of magnitude lower than those observed for zincfinger peptides. Generally, in these early studies, only first-shell effects (direct ligand effects) were considered. Recently, however, the effects of secondary ligands have begun to be examined, increasing our understanding of the importance of the positioning of the metal-binding site within a framework of additional functional groups.
Marino and Regan $[30^{\bullet\bullet}]$ engineered a tetrahedral zinc-binding site into the B1 domain of IgG-binding protein G, and then extended the design concept by the incorporation of secondary ligands. The intended role of these incorporated functional groups was to establish a hydrogen-bonding network to stabilize the positions of the metal-coordinating histidine residues while retaining the desired coordination geometry. The authors report that the incorporated mutations increased the protein’s affinity for metal and that introducing multiple mutations produced additive effects $[30^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . These results not only demonstrate the overall importance of secondary ligand interactions to the properties of a metal-binding site, but also represent a crucial step towards engineering sites for more complex properties such as catalysis.
Investigations into the nature of the dual function exhibited by $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in biological systems will probably also increase our understanding of metal–protein interactions, proteinfolding dynamics, and the role of secondary ligands. What aspects of a zinc-binding site (and indeed the local protein structure) determine whether the zinc ion that occupies that site will behave in a catalytic or a purely structural role? Experimental evidence obtained by redesigning existing zinc-binding sites indicates that zinc functionality in a protein center depends on more than the zinc ligandenvironment — four protein ligands for structural zinc versus three protein ligands and an activated water molecule or hydroxide ligand for catalytic zinc.
In one study by Berg and co-workers [31], a truncated zincfinger was employed in an attempt to convert a structural zinc into a catalytic zinc by opening up a coordination site to enable the binding of a catalytic water molecule. Although the authors were able to demonstrate that a water molecule was likely to be bound in the fourth coordinate position, this designed complex exhibited no catalytic activity. Additionally, in a related study, the catalytic zinc site of a carbonic anhydrase (CA) enzyme was rendered inactive by engineering a fourth zinc ligand into the zinc-binding site, although the metal affinity was increased by over two orders of magnitude [32].
More recently, the second-shell contributions from the highly conserved hydrophobic residues around the zinc binding site in human CAII have been examined $[33^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The phenylalanine and tryptophan residues that stabilize the metal-binding histidine residues in CAII contribute to the high zinc-affinity and slow zinc-dissociation rate constant. Substitution of these secondary ligands with smaller sidechain amino-acid residues, such as methionine, valine, isoleucine and/or serine, produced a decrease in both the affinity for cobalt and zinc and the overall stability of the folded protein $[33^{\bullet\bullet}]$ .
## Conclusions
The autonomous nature of the classical zinc-finger has made it an ideal model system for examining the relationship between metal binding and protein folding. The relative simplicity of this system has enabled researchers to evaluate the effects of both direct and indirect ligands on the overall stability of a well-defined structural element. Increases in our understanding of these so-called first-shell and second-shell effects are forthcoming, and the potential implications in the area of protein design/redesign are many. Already, the concept of metal-binding-site design and incorporation has been extended to include the functional contributions from sidechains not directly involved in metal binding. Studies of catalytic-zinc binding sites have also been extended to explore the role of second-shell interactions. These types of studies, which contribute only in a peripheral sense to our understanding of the true biological role of zinc fingers, nonetheless demonstrate the importance of this protein structure motif. Using the zinc-finger motif to evaluate the thermodynamic parameters that govern the coupled processes of metal binding and protein folding and as a template in protein design/redesign, will provide insight into factors that lead to high metal-ion affinity as well as aid in the design of de novo catalytic sites and the in vivo application of zinc-based probes.
